00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.110 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon everyone i'm happy to be  here to provide today's updates on  covid19 and let me begin with our latest  numbers  i'm pleased to share that almost all our  indicators continue to trend downward  after a difficult fifth wave that  brought so much uncertainty over the  past few months this is very welcome  news  over the last 24 hours we have  identified 791  new cases of coven 19 and completed  about 2 800 tests  this puts our positivity rate for lab  confirmed cases  at about 27 percent  which is nearly a 14 drop since last  week  there are 11 189  active cases in the province and that  number has also been decreasing  in fact there are 68 percent fewer  active cases today compared to the  beginning of february sadly over the  past 24 hours 13 new deaths were  reported to alberta health  i extend my deepest condolences and  sympathies to the families and friends  of those albertans  another declining trend is a number of  schools that have had to shift to online  learning to address operational  challenges  albert education has confirmed that as  of today there are no schools  in temporary at-home learning  looking to hospitalizations  one week ago there were 1500 albertans  in hospital which  that  with or due to coven 19  including 121 in icu  today there are a total of  1373 in hospital including 90 in icu  this decrease is a positive sign that  pressure is easing on our health care  system and hopefully a beginning of  relief for our hard-working health care  workers  they're under strain and i feel for them  and i am grateful to them  but even with the strain they've been  under for the past two years  they continue to do a remarkable job for  the patients that they serve  ahs is currently completing about 95  percent  of normal surgical volume  and my department and i are supporting  them to get back to 100  as soon as possible and then to go even  higher  and it's not just surgery  our dedicated health care staff are  keeping the system working at high level  in other areas as well  in cancer care patients are getting  access to treatment within about the  same time that they did before the  pandemic  even in continuing care where there's  been a particular strain on the  workforce services are there  for patients waiting in hospital for a  continuing care bed the average wait  time is actually shorter now than it was  before the pandemic  our continuing care staff and operators  are working incredibly hard to maintain  access  and your government is working hard to  support them  the health system is still there for  patients who need it  and it's a strong foundation to build on  as we get past the omicron wave  and work to improve access and wait  times  and reduce wait times not just restore  the system to the level we had before  the pandemic  but give albertans better access to  surgery diagnostic tests and other care  right across our health care system  as we look towards our next stage in  easing measures i know that some  albertans continue to be concerned about  protecting themselves from covet 19  but the science is clear the single most  effective way to protect ourselves and  each other is by getting vaccinated and  this includes ensuring albertans  continue to get their booster shots  in alberta while 86.8 percent of the  adult population has received at least  two doses of the vaccine  only 44.9 percent has received a third  dose  that means there are still hundreds of  thousands of people who haven't yet  received one  we need to close the gap to help keep  hospitalizations low  as we prepare to further ease measures  so if you haven't gotten your butcher  shot yet i encourage you to do so today  one of my key priorities is to get our  vaccination coverage higher and that  includes removing barriers to receiving  vaccines  as well as answering questions and  urging people to make the choice to get  vaccinated if they haven't done so yet  teams at alberta health and alberta  health services continue to look for  ways to make getting vaccinated easier  for albertans of all ages  currently 47.2 percent  of children and youth have received at  least one dose and only 24 percent have  received two doses  we believe that by easing the burden on  working parents and guardians of booking  arranging and getting to vaccination  appointments we'll see an increase in  the number of young albertans between  the ages of 5 and 11 years  who are protected against covet 19.  that's why i'm pleased to announce that  we're increasing the options at the 97  ahs clinics offering  offering pediatric vaccine across the  province  for march 2nd to 16  ahs clinics across the province will  offer walk-in options including evenings  and weekends with some clinics beginning  to offer walk-in doses starting this  week  the clinics will operate from 3 pm to 8  pm monday to friday and noon till 6 pm  on saturday and sunday  after march 16  demand will be assessed to determine if  the expanded services should be  continued  that being said appointments are  encouraged and will still be honored at  all ahs clinics  in addition i am pleased to announce  that there will be more pharmacies  offering pediatric vaccines  to create additional access points for  albertans to have their children  vaccinated  17 pharmacies and rural communities will  be offering pediatric vaccines and will  add pharmacies in edmonton calgary  red deer lethbridge grand prairie  medicine hat and fort mcmurray  approximately 150 pharmacies will be  asked to participate based on their  experience in immunizing children and  they'll be ready to start as early as  march the second  finally i'm pleased to say that a number  of physicians will also be helping to  vaccinate the youngest eligible  albertans from march 2nd to the 16th  about 30 physicians have expressed  interest in administering vaccines and  their office will begin to receive  supply soon  and any other family physicians  interested in participating will also be  eligible to provide children's vaccines  i know that many albertans continue to  weigh the pros and cons of vaccinating  their kids and i hope that increased  availability helps to make this decision  easier  as we near the two-year milestone of  covid arriving in alberta i want to  recognize what an incredibly challenging  couple of years it has been for everyone  the current omicron wave is passing  and we and other jurisdictions are  returning to a more normal life  but i urge albertans to continue to  follow the rules that are still in place  this is especially critical in  continuing care settings where enhanced  measures will remain in place even once  we're into step two  please respect those rules and do your  part to help keep those vulnerable  residents safe  while they receive the care that they  need  we want to continue to support each  other through this transition and that  means respecting each other's choices  and accepting that people will  transition at their own pace  so thank you and i'll now happy to take  questions  thanks minister we've got a couple  reporters on the phone so uh we'll go to  the first caller please  the first caller is kerry tate from  global mail please go ahead  hi thanks for taking my call minister  i'm wondering if you have a goal for  childhood vaccination rates in light of  the  push in march  yeah we haven't set a specific target  but we would like to increase it and and  you know move closer and even achieve  the average across the country  we do know that you know when initial  surveys were done  you know  roughly one-third of the parents in  alberta were interested in getting their  kids vaccinated we've surpassed that but  i do know that you know when we compare  ourselves to other jurisdiction uh we  are one of the lowest in the country uh  and i'd like to actually you know reach  the the average for uh for the country  and this is one step in that direction  is providing easier access for parents  to get their kids vaccinated  thanks minister follow-up carrie  sure minister why do you think albert uh  parents in alberta are more reluctant  yeah  really great question um you know we've  seen that before and even in terms of  the initial surveys that were done that  there was tend to be more more reluctant  i i think honestly some parents are  taking a a wait and see attitude in  terms of you know we'll uh we'll see you  know how the rollout goes  and i also understand that some parents  um who have seen you know their kids get  uh coveted with the omicron wave which  has been so prevalent throughout the  province um at this point time may be  saying okay i'm going to hold back if  they haven't gotten vaccinated but again  i would you know urge all parents at the  best protection um even if someone has  actually had covet is to to get  vaccinated  and urge them to do so and and hopefully  the changes we announced here will make  it easier and increase the vaccination  rates  thank you  thanks minister we'll go to the next  question please  the first question is from elise tilty  tvc  please go ahead  hi minister it's actually a non-coveted  question for you sorry but um your  expert advisory panel last year  recommended a shift to more  community-based care for elders and  seniors your government has said it's  working on a plan to address that  can you tell me do you expect to see  significant changes this year on that  file and if so what should we be  watching for in tomorrow's budget  yeah so a couple of things um  you know as you know we did a  facility-based continuing care review  that was done last year uh government is  working on a response to that and you're  quite right as part of that review has  looked at more community-based care  and and also you know uh be able to  expand  our continuing care system so when we  moving forward  we we have legislation continuing care  legislation that we'll be introducing  this session  and that's going to help us renew our  reg our legal and regulatory framework  because right now we have different uh  acts that apply to  the continuing care setting um and it's  it's across the field it's very complex  and it's difficult to uh difficult to  manage so we're bringing that all under  one act and then over the course of the  next year our focus will be is getting  the regulations right  so we can actually move towards a policy  environment that supports care at home  because that's what we heard loud and  clear in addition um you know we we've  talked about before our need to support  the continuing care sector uh and and as  well as there's going to be an increased  demand um basically for more continuing  care spaces so i'm not going to jump the  gun for tomorrow but  you you will see a focus on us in terms  of expanding continuing care spaces uh  over the next year  thanks minister uh elise follow up  thanks yeah i appreciate that um  as you know with the and you mentioned  there's a capital investment that's been  recommended and that included some new  models like these small care facilities  at the same time advocates have been  working with your government to look at  what additional programs in the  community that seniors would need to  stay at home longer do you expect most  of the funding will be for experiments  and research in that area or are you  actually getting ready to roll out some  of these new programs and grant at a  larger scale this year  yeah so we'll have to wait for after the  budget but again our focus is on getting  the policy uh environment correct uh and  then looking at new model like capital  and then also looking at new models to  be able to provide services to albertans  particularly  those who would actually otherwise need  continuing care continuing care spaces  thanks minister so we had just three  callers uh initially so we'll we'll take  just one more the last of the three  next question is from kevin mimick ctp  calibri please go ahead  hi minister you announced new covet  deaths reported in the past 24 hours but  we still haven't heard about reported  deaths from the previous 96 hours so how  many deaths did alberta health record  between friday and monday  yeah so the those numbers will be  updated actually i think they're being  updated on the site as we speak um in  terms of the the other numbers for the  last four days so if you just go to the  site you can you can see them there and  they're being confirmed as we speak  thanks minister kevin follow-up  yeah i'm on the site and they're not  here um i'll keep checking but could you  elaborate a bit more on what the  technical issues are that your team's  facing  yeah so there is a detailed amount of  assessment that that  that  you know the teams that double check um  they were able to do it very quickly for  today's update uh for 24 hours uh but  doing over four day period uh my  understanding there's a significant  amount of work that to do that  my thought it was actually uh done and  they're updating it the site right right  now um but it should be done within uh  within uh the next few minutes uh and  we'll and then if not we can actually  get that to you through uh our press  secretary steve thank you  thanks minister i have thanks to the  callers there were just three today so  that wraps it up for today thanks again  minister thank you everyone and have a  great afternoon

